        "granularity": "NARRATIVE_ATOMIC",
        "id": "claim:graph-theoretic-nonstabilizerness:perfect-graph-closed-form",
        "normalized_statement": "The paper proves that, when the measurement set has no active dependencies and its frustration graph is perfect, reduced RoM has the clique closed form max(1, max over cliques Q of the sum over P in Q of |tr(P rho)|); the paper further presents finite-precision evaluation as polynomial-time maximum-weight clique optimization on a perfect graph.",
        "occurrences": [
          {
            "id": "occurrence:2607-perfect-graph-closed-form:1",
            "line_end": 100,
            "line_start": 95,
            "occurrence_role": "PRIMARY",
            "source_artifact": {
              "path": "Stabilizerness/arXiv-2607.26154v1/draft.tex",
              "sha256": "sha256:4a84425a4d56efe5b5bee4814a09c99996ab7a6bd279574d567979c248f81c1d"
            },
            "source_characterization": {
              "conditionality": "org.agtxiv.conditionality.conditional",
              "formality": "org.agtxiv.formality.narrative",
              "speech_act": "org.agtxiv.speech_act.derives"
            },
            "source_text": "\tWe prove that this relaxation is exact if and only if $\\Mcal$ has no active dependencies, where an active dependency is a minimal Pauli product relation contained entirely in an independent set, as depicted schematically in Fig.~\\ref{fig:graph-polytope-rom}.\n\tUnder this relaxation, the separation oracle for the dual constraints reduces to a standard maximum-weight independent set (MWIS) problem on $G_\\Mcal$.\n\tExploiting the polynomial-time solvability of MWIS on perfect graphs, the evaluation of $\\RoM_\\Mcal(\\rho)$ is further reduced to an efficiently computable closed form in the absence of active dependencies.\n\t\n\tBeyond evaluating $\\RoM_\\Mcal(\\rho)$, the closed form determines the witness capacity of a measurement set $\\Mcal$, defined as $\\sup_\\rho\\RoM_\\Mcal(\\rho)$.\n\tIn the solvable regime, the witness capacity equals $\\sqrt{\\clnum(G_\\Mcal)}$, where the clique number $\\clnum(G_\\Mcal)$ is the size of the largest pairwise anticommuting subset of $\\Mcal$.",
            "source_zone": "org.agtxiv.source_zone.introduction"
          },
          {
            "id": "occurrence:2607-perfect-graph-closed-form:2",
            "line_end": 283,
            "line_start": 270,
            "occurrence_role": "BODY_SUPPORT",
            "source_artifact": {
              "path": "Stabilizerness/arXiv-2607.26154v1/draft.tex",
              "sha256": "sha256:4a84425a4d56efe5b5bee4814a09c99996ab7a6bd279574d567979c248f81c1d"
            },
            "source_characterization": {
              "conditionality": "org.agtxiv.conditionality.conditional",
              "formality": "org.agtxiv.formality.formal_statement",
              "speech_act": "org.agtxiv.speech_act.proves"
            },
            "source_text": "\t\\begin{theorem}\n\t\t\\label{thm:solvable}\n\t\tIf $\\Mcal$ has no active dependencies and $G_\\Mcal$ is perfect, then for any $n$-qubit quantum state $\\rho$,\n\t\t{\\small\n\t\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:closed-form}\n\t\t\t\t\\RoM_\\Mcal\\big(\\rho\\big)\n\t\t\t\t=\n\t\t\t\t\\max\\!\\Big(1,\\ \\max_{Q} \\sum_{P\\in Q}\\big|\\tr(P\\rho)\\big| \\Big)\n\t\t\t\t\\le\\sqrt{\\clnum(G_\\Mcal)},\n\t\t\t\\end{equation}\n\t\t}\n\t\twhere $Q$ ranges over cliques of $G_\\Mcal$, equivalently over pairwise anticommuting subsets of $\\Mcal$.\n\t\tThe bound is attained by any state supported on the $+1$ eigenspace of $|Q|^{-1/2}\\sum_{P\\in Q}P$ for the maximum clique $Q$.\n\t\\end{theorem}",
            "source_zone": "org.agtxiv.source_zone.theorem"
          },
          {
            "id": "occurrence:2607-perfect-graph-closed-form:3",
            "line_end": 284,
            "line_start": 284,
            "occurrence_role": "BODY_SUPPORT",
            "source_artifact": {
              "path": "Stabilizerness/arXiv-2607.26154v1/draft.tex",
              "sha256": "sha256:4a84425a4d56efe5b5bee4814a09c99996ab7a6bd279574d567979c248f81c1d"
            },
            "source_characterization": {
              "conditionality": "org.agtxiv.conditionality.conditional",
              "formality": "org.agtxiv.formality.narrative",
              "speech_act": "org.agtxiv.speech_act.explains"
            },
            "source_text": "\tFor finite precision expectation values, evaluating Eq.~\\eqref{eq:closed-form} is a maximum-weight clique problem with weights $|\\tr(P\\rho)|$, equivalently MWIS on complement graph $\\overline{G_\\Mcal}$. The complement of a perfect graph is perfect, so Lemma~\\ref{lem:perfect-collapse} in Appendix~\\ref{app:proof-mwis} gives a polynomial-time evaluation.",
            "source_zone": "org.agtxiv.source_zone.body"
          }
        ],
